hadiths[36]{hadithnumber,text}:
"1633.1","Malik narrated to me from Zuraik ibn Hakim al-Ayli that a man named Misbah employed his son, and it seemed he found him slow. When the son came, he said to him, ""O adulterer!"" Zuraik said: I was asked to flog him (for false accusation). When I intended to flog him, his son swore: ""By Allah, if you flog him, I will confess to zina against myself."" When he said that, the matter became problematic for me. I wrote to 'Umar ibn 'Abd al-'Aziz — who was the governor at that time — mentioning this. 'Umar wrote back to me: ""Accept his pardon."" Zuraik said: I also wrote to 'Umar ibn 'Abd al-'Aziz: ""What about a man who is falsely accused, or his parents, and they have died or one of them?"" 'Umar wrote back: ""If he pardons, accept his pardon regarding himself. If the false accusation was against his parents and they have died or one of them, then exact the right for him by the Book of Allah, unless he desires concealment."" Yahya said: I heard Malik say: This means that the falsely accused man fears that if the matter is exposed, evidence might be established against him. If he is as described and he pardons, his pardon is valid."
"1633.2","Yahya related to me from Malik that Muadh ibn Jabal said"
