"1646.2","Yahya related to me from Malik from Ibn Shihab from Anas ibn Malik that the Messenger of Allah"
"1647.1","Malik narrated to me that Abu al-Zinad informed him that a governor of 'Umar ibn 'Abd al-'Aziz seized people for hiraba (armed robbery) but they had not killed anyone. He wanted to cut off their hands or kill them, so he wrote to 'Umar ibn 'Abd al-'Aziz about it. 'Umar wrote back: ""If you took the lesser of the two (punishments)."" Yahya said: I heard Malik say: The ruling with us concerning one who steals people's goods placed in the markets, secured in their containers and bundled together: whoever steals from that security something whose value reaches the threshold for cutting, his hand is cut, whether the owner was present at his goods or not, night or day. Malik said concerning one who steals something liable to cutting, then the stolen goods are found with him and returned to the owner: his hand is cut. Malik said: If someone says, ""How can his hand be cut when the goods were taken from him and returned to the owner?"" It is like the drinker from whom the smell of intoxicant is found but he is not intoxicated: he is flogged the hadd. The hadd for intoxicants is applied when he drinks it, even if it does not intoxicate him, because he drank it to become intoxicated. Similarly, the thief's hand is cut for the theft he committed, even if he did not benefit from it and it returned to its owner, because he stole it intending to take it away. Malik said concerning a group who come to a house and steal together, carrying out the goods jointly — a chest, a beam, a basket, or similar — if they remove it from its security jointly and its value reaches the cutting threshold (three dirhams or more), they all have their hands cut. If each carries out separate goods, whoever carries goods worth three dirhams or more has his hand cut; whoever does not, has no cutting. Yahya said: Malik said: The ruling with us is that if a man's house is locked and he is alone in it, no cutting is due on one who steals from it until he takes it out of the entire house, because the whole house is its security. If there is another dweller in the house, each locking his own door, and they are all secure, whoever steals from one of those rooms something liable to cutting and brings it out into the house has removed it from its security to a non-security, and cutting is due. Malik said: The ruling with us concerning a slave who steals from his master's goods from his master's goods: if he is not of his servants nor of those trusted with his house, but enters secretly and steals something liable to cutting, there is no cutting on him. Likewise a slave girl who steals from her master's goods: no cutting. But if a slave not of his servants nor trusted enters secretly and steals from his master's wife's goods something liable to cutting, his hand is cut. Likewise a woman's slave girl who is not her servant nor her husband's nor trusted with the house, enters secretly and steals from her mistress's goods something liable to cutting: no cutting. But if she steals from her mistress's husband's goods something liable to cutting, her hand is cut. Likewise a man who steals from his wife's goods or a woman from her husband's goods something liable to cutting: if the theft was from a house other than the one they both lock and was in a security other than the house they are in, cutting is due. Malik said concerning a small child and a mute person who cannot speak: if they are stolen from their security or lock, the thief's hand is cut. If they wander out of their security or lock, there is no cutting on the thief. They are like a mountain guard or hanging fruit. Malik said: The ruling with us concerning one who exhumes graves: if what he takes from the grave reaches the cutting threshold, his hand is cut. The grave is a security for what is in it, just as houses are a security for what is in them. Cutting is not due until he brings it out of the grave."
"1647.2","Yahya related to me from Malik from Abu'z-Zinad from al-Araj from Abu Hurayra that the Messenger of Allah"
